We introduce a straightforward yet surprising criterion for determining the boundedness of Schur multipliers $S_M$ on Schatten $p$-classes, thereby resolving a conjecture by Mikael de la Salle. For $1 < p < \infty$, our primary result, expressed for matrices on $\mathbf{R}^n \times \mathbf{R}^n$, takes the form:
$$\big\| S_M: S_p \to S_p \big\|_{\mathrm{cb}} \lesssim \frac{p^2}{p-1}
\sum_{|\gamma| \le [\frac{n}{2}] +1} \Big\| |x-y|^{|\gamma|} \Big\{ \big|
\partial_x^\gamma M(x,y) \big| + \big| \partial_y^\gamma M(x,y) \big| \Big\}
\Big\|_\infty.$$
This formulation represents a comprehensive matrix amplification of the H\"ormander-Mikhlin multiplier theorem (applicable to non-Toeplitz and non-trigonometric settings) that accommodates fractional differentiability orders $\sigma > \frac{n}{2}$. The result naturally encompasses Arazy's conjecture regarding $S_p$-multipliers and generalizes it to $\alpha$-divided differences. Furthermore, it yields novel Littlewood-Paley characterizations for $S_p$-norms and facilitates significant applications within harmonic analysis concerning nilpotent and high-rank simple Lie group algebras.

The LHCb experiment analyzes $pp$ collision data with integrated luminosities of 9 fb$^{-1}$ or 6 fb$^{-1}$ to investigate $CP$ violation in the two-body decays $D_{(s)}^{+}\rightarrow h^{+}\pi^{0}$ and $D_{(s)}^{+} \rightarrow h^{+}\eta$, where $h^{+}$ represents either a $\pi^{+}$ or $K^{+}$ meson. The $\pi^{0}$ and $\eta$ mesons are identified through the $e^{+}e^{-}\gamma$ final state, arising from either three-body decays ($\pi^{0}\rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}\gamma$ and $\eta \rightarrow e^{+}e^{-}\gamma$) or two-body decays ($\pi^{0}\rightarrow \gamma\gamma$ and $\eta\rightarrow\gamma\gamma$) followed by photon conversion. To mitigate production and detection asymmetries, these measurements are normalized to the control channels $D_{(s)}^{+}\rightarrow K_{S}^{0} h^{+}$. The resulting $CP$ asymmetries are reported as follows:

\begin{align} \mathcal{A}_{CP}(D^{+}\rightarrow \pi^{+}\pi^{0})
&= (-1.3 \pm 0.9 \pm 0.6 )\%, \end{align} \begin{align}
\mathcal{A}_{CP}(D^{+}\rightarrow K^{+}\pi^{0}) &= (-3.2 \pm 4.7 \pm 2.1 )\%,
\end{align} \begin{align} \mathcal{A}_{CP}(D^{+}\rightarrow \pi^{+}\eta) &=
(-0.2 \pm 0.8 \pm 0.4 )\%, \end{align} \begin{align}
\mathcal{A}_{CP}(D^{+}\rightarrow K^{+}\eta) &= (-6 \pm 10 \pm 4 )\%,
\end{align} \begin{align} \mathcal{A}_{CP}(D_{s}^{+}\rightarrow K^{+}\pi^{0})
&= (-0.8 \pm 3.9 \pm 1.2 )\%, \end{align} \begin{align}
\mathcal{A}_{CP}(D_{s}^{+}\rightarrow \pi^{+}\eta) &= (0.8 \pm 0.7 \pm 0.5 )\%,
\end{align} \begin{align} \mathcal{A}_{CP}(D_{s}^{+}\rightarrow K^{+}\eta) &=
(0.9 \pm 3.7 \pm 1.1 )\%, \end{align}

where the first uncertainties reflect statistical variations and the second systematic ones. These findings are consistent with the absence of $CP$ violation and represent the most precise determinations of $\mathcal{A}_{CP}$ for these specific decay modes available to date.

The primary focus of this work is the concept of a sequentially compact space $X$ belonging to FinBW($\mathcal{I}$), where $\mathcal{I}$ is an ideal on $\omega$. This membership is defined such that for any sequence $(x_n)$ in $X$, there exists a set $A 
otin \mathcal{I}$ for which the subsequence $(x_n)_{n \in A}$ converges within $X$. We introduce an ideal $\mathcal{BI}$ that is pivotal for FinBW($\mathcal{I}$), characterized by the following equivalence: for any ideal $\mathcal{I}$, the condition $\mathcal{BI} 
ot\leq_K \mathcal{I}$ (where $\leq_K$ represents the Katětov preorder) holds if and only if, assuming the Continuum Hypothesis (CH), there exists an uncountable separable space in FinBW($\mathcal{I}$). We demonstrate that for all $ f{\Pi^0_4}$ ideals $\mathcal{I}$, the inequality $\mathcal{BI} 
ot\leq_K \mathcal{I}$ holds and that $\omega_1$ equipped with the order topology is an element of FinBW($\mathcal{I}$). Furthermore, we investigate the non-emptiness of the difference set FinBW($\mathcal{I}$) $\setminus$ FinBW($\mathcal{J}$). Under the assumption of MA($\sigma$-centered), we prove that for $ f{\Pi^0_4}$ ideals $\mathcal{I}$ and $\mathcal{J}$, this set is nonempty precisely when $\mathcal{J} 
ot\leq_K \mathcal{I}$. Additionally, providing a negative answer to a question posed by M. Hrušák and D. Meza-Alcántara, we establish that the ideal $	ext{Fin}	imes	ext{Fin}$ is not critical among Borel ideals regarding extendability to a $ f{\Pi^0_3}$ ideal. Finally, we apply these findings to the analysis of Hindman spaces and within the framework of analytic P-ideals.

Theoretical investigations explore the in-plane phonon-drag thermopower ($S^g$), diffusion thermopower ($S^d$), and power factor ($PF$) in twisted bilayer graphene (tBLG) across varying twist angles ($	heta$), temperatures ($T$), and electron densities ($n_s$), focusing on the low-temperature range of 1–20 K. As the twist angle approaches the magic angle ($	heta_m$), both $S^g$ and $S^d$ exhibit significant enhancement, reflecting the substantial suppression of the Fermi velocity (${
u_F}^*$) within the moiré flat band near $	heta_m$. This enhancement diminishes as either $	heta$ or $T$ increases. Within the Bloch-Grüneisen regime, $S^g$ scales with ${
u_F}^{* -2}$, $T^3$, and ${n_s}^{-1/2}$. With rising temperature, the exponent $\delta$ in the relation $S^g \sim T^\delta$ shifts from 3 to nearly zero, yielding an estimated peak $S^g$ of approximately 10 mV/K around 20 K. In terms of density, $S^g$ is larger for smaller $n_s$ at low temperatures but smaller for smaller $n_s$ at high temperatures. Conversely, $S^d$, described by the Mott formula, scales as ${
u_F}^{* -1}$, $T$, and ${n_s}^{-1/2}$, remaining significantly lower than $S^g$ for $T > \sim$ 2 K. The power factor $PF$ also demonstrates strong dependence on $	heta$ and marked enhancement. These findings suggest the potential for a giant figure of merit. In tBLG, $	heta$ serves as a powerful tuning parameter for $S^g$, $S^d$, and $PF$, alongside $T$ and $n_s$. Finally, the results are qualitatively compared with experimental measurements of out-of-plane thermopower in tBLG.

