This research investigates the global (in time) existence of small data solutions for damped $\sigma$-evolution equations featuring nonlocal (in space) nonlinearities, described by:
\begin{equation*}
\partial_{t}^{2}u+(-\Delta)^{\sigma}u+\partial_{t}u+(-\Delta)^{\sigma}\partial_{t}u=I_{\alpha}(|u|^{p}), \ \ t>0, \ \ x\in \mathbb{R}^{n},
\end{equation*}
where $\sigma\geq1$, $p>1$, and $I_{\alpha}$ denotes the Riesz potential acting on the power nonlinearity $|u|^{p}$ for any $\alpha\in (0,n)$. Specifically, by employing $(L^{m}\cap L^{2})-L^{2}$ and $L^{2}-L^{2}$ linear estimates with $m\in[1,2]$, we demonstrate the novel impact of the parameter $\alpha$ on the permissible ranges for the exponent $p$.

This article investigates a fractional $p$-Laplacian equation featuring a singular nonlinearity, defined as:
\begin{equation*}
(P_{\la}) \left\{ \begin{array}{lr} - 2\int_{\mb
R^n}\frac{|w(y)-w(x)|^{p-2}(w(y)-w(x))}{|x-y|^{n+ps}}dy = a(x) w^{-q}+ \la b(x)
w^r\; \text{in}\; \Om \quad \quad w>0\;\text{in}\;\Om, \quad w = 0 \;
\mbox{in}\; \mb R^n \setminus\Om, \end{array} \quad \right. \end{equation*}
In this context, $\Om$ represents a bounded domain within $\mb R^n$ possessing a smooth boundary $\partial \Om$, satisfying the condition $n> ps$ where $s\in(0,1)$. The parameters are specified as $\la>0$, $0<q<1$, and $q<p-1<r< p_{s}^*-1$, with $p_{s}^*=\frac{np}{n-ps}$. The coefficient functions $a: \Om\subset\mb R^n \ra \mb R$ and $b: \Om\subset\mb R^n \ra \mb R$ belong to $L^{\frac{p^{*}_{s}}{p^{*}_{s}-1+q}}(\Om)$ and $L^{\frac{p^{*}_{s}}{p^{*}_{s}-1-r}}(\Om)$ respectively, where $b(x)$ is sign-changing and $a(x)$ satisfies $0< a(x)$. By employing variational techniques, the study establishes the existence and multiplicity of positive solutions for $(P_{\la})$ relative to the parameter $\la$.

This study reports the initial observation of $B^0_{(s)} \to J/\psi p \overline{p}$ decays, utilizing $5.2{\text{\,fb}}^{-1}$ of proton-proton collision data acquired by the \mbox{LHCb}\xspace detector. These decay modes are naturally suppressed by both limited phase space and Okubo-Zweig-Iizuka or Cabibbo mechanisms. The measured branching fractions are given by:
\begin{align*} \mathcal{B}(B^0 \to J/\psi p \overline{p}) &= (4.51\pm 0.40\; \text{(stat)} \pm 0.44\; \text{(syst)}) \times 10^{-7}, \\ \mathcal{B}(B^0_s \to J/\psi p \overline{p}) &= (3.58\pm 0.19\; \text{(stat)} \pm 0.39\; \text{(syst)}) \times 10^{-6}. \end{align*}
Notably, the result for the $B^0_s$ meson significantly exceeds the anticipated value of ${\cal O}(10^{-9})$. Furthermore, the constrained phase space in these decays enables the most precise single measurements to date of the $B^0$ mass, determined as ${5279.74 \pm 0.30\; ({\rm stat})\pm 0.10\; ({\rm syst})}$ MeV, and the $B^0_s$ mass, determined as ${5366.85 \pm 0.19\; ({\rm stat})\pm 0.13\; ({\rm syst})}$ MeV.

This study investigates the existence and asymptotic behavior of least energy solutions for the fractional eigenvalue problem $(P)$:
\begin{equation*}
(P)\quad (-\Delta)^{s}u+V(x)u=\mu u+am(x)|u|^{\frac{4s}{N}}u,\quad
\int_{\mathbb{R}^{N}}|u|^{2}dx=1,\ u\in H^{s}(\mathbb{R}^{N}),
\end{equation*}
where $s\in(0,1)$, $\mu\in\mathbb{R}$, $a>0$, and $V(x)$, $m(x)$ are bounded functions on $\mathbb{R}^{N}$ with $N\geq2$. We establish the existence of a critical threshold $a_s^{*}>0$ such that for every $a\in(0,a_s^{*})$, problem $(P)$ admits a least energy solution $u_{a}(x)$. As $a$ approaches $a_s^{*}$ from below, $u_{a}$ exhibits blow-up at a specific point $x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^N$ where $V(x)$ attains its minimum and $m(x)$ attains its maximum. Furthermore, we derive precise blow-up rates for $u_a$ under appropriate conditions imposed on $V(x)$ and $m(x)$.

