e 0)$, and $V_3$, which produce constructive interference with the Standard Model. While the event excess could theoretically be (partially) attributed to representations $	ilde{S}_2, V_1\,(\kappa_1^R
e 0), V_2\,(\kappa_2^{LR}
e 0),$ and $	ilde{V}_2$—which interfere destructively—this would only work in high invariant mass bins where new physics dominates; however, such scenarios demand large couplings that are ruled out by other observables. Furthermore, the representations $S_1$ and $V_1\, (\kappa_1^{L} 
e 0)$ fail to enhance the fit to the CMS data beyond the Standard Model.

Let $\Omega\subset \mathbb{R}^n $ be an arbitrary open set and $u$ be a weak supersolution of the equation $\mathcal{L}u=c(x)g(|u|)\frac{u}{|u|}$, where the operator $\mathcal{L}$ is defined by
\[\mathcal{L}u(x)=\text{p.v.} \int_{\mathbb{R}^n}
g\left(\frac{|u(x)-u(y)|}{|x-y|^s}\right) \frac{u(x)-u(y)}{|u(x)-u(y)|}
K(x,y)\frac{dy}{|x-y|^s}\]
and $g$ is the derivative of a Young function $G$. This note establishes a Hopf-type lemma and a strong minimum principle for $u$ under the assumption that $c(x)$ is continuous on $\bar{\Omega}$, thereby extending the findings of Del Pezzo and Quaas (JDE-2017) to the fractional Orlicz-Sobolev framework.

This paper categorizes all positive solutions to the integral equation $ u(x)=\int_{\mathbb{R}^n_+}K_b(x,y)y_n^b f(u(y))dy $, where $ b > 1 $ is a constant. The kernel $ K_b(x,y) $ represents the Green function associated with the following homogeneous Neumann boundary problem:
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \begin{aligned} -\text{div}(x^{b}_n \nabla u)&= f \quad in
\mathbb{R}^n_+ \\ \frac{\partial u}{\partial x_n}&= 0 \quad on \ \partial
\mathbb{R}^n_+ . \end{aligned} \right. \end{equation}
By employing the moving planes method in integral form, we demonstrate the symmetry of these positive solutions. Furthermore, we prove the equivalence between the integral equation and its corresponding partial differential equation. Additionally, these findings can be extended to integral systems.

